---
title: Defining an event handler
description: IEventHandler — many handlers per event, what happens when one throws, and why handlers must not depend on each other.
---

An event handler implements `IEventHandler<TEvent>`. There is no result to return:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    private readonly IMailer _mailer;

    public SendWelcomeEmail(IMailer mailer) => _mailer = mailer;

    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => _mailer.SendAsync(@event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Any number of handlers can exist for one event — that is the point of the module. Adding a
second one is not a configuration error, unlike with commands and queries.

## Handlers must be independent

Handlers for the same event run in a defined order (direct before covariant, then `[Weight]`
descending), but you should not build on that order. Two rules follow:

- **Do not pass data between handlers.** If handler B needs what handler A produced, they are
  one operation and belong in one handler — or A should be a command that publishes the event
  B reacts to.
- **Do not assume the others succeeded.** A handler that must run only after another
  succeeded is describing a workflow, not a broadcast.

The item bag on the context does let handlers share data within one dispatch, which makes
this easy to violate. Reserve it for
[interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/interceptors) passing data to the pipeline, not
for handler-to-handler communication.

## When a handler throws

The exception propagates out of `PublishAsync`, and the
[exception stage](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors) runs. That
means one failing subscriber can fail the whole publish — and, if the publish is inside a
command handler, the command with it.

When a subscriber's failure genuinely should not fail the publisher, contain it in the
handler:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    try
    {
        await _searchIndex.AddAsync(@event.Id, @event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
    }
    catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not OperationCanceledException)
    {
        _logger.LogError(ex, "Indexing {Product} failed; continuing", @event.Id);
    }
}
```

Letting cancellation through is deliberate — a cancelled dispatch should stay cancelled.

For work that must survive process failure, do not do it in the handler at all: write to an
outbox in the handler and let a separate worker do the delivery.

## Covariant handlers

A handler registered against a base type or interface receives every assignable event:

```csharp
public sealed class AppendToOutbox : IEventHandler<IDomainEvent>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(IDomainEvent @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => _outbox.AppendAsync(@event, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Handlers registered for the concrete event type run first, then covariant ones. This is what
lets an outbox, an audit sink or a metrics collector subscribe to a whole family of events
with a single class.

## Lifetimes

Event handlers resolve from the calling DI scope on each dispatch, like every other handler.
A handler holding a `DbContext` should be **scoped**. See
[Configuring Ergosfare](/ergosfare.docs/getting-started/configuring#handler-lifetimes).

Because a publish may fan out to many handlers, this is the module where transient handlers
allocate most visibly — one instance per handler per dispatch. Register them as singletons
when they are stateless.

## Publishing from a handler

Use a [nested scope](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/nested-dispatch) so the inner dispatch gets
a clean item bag and the parent's cancellation token:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderPlaced @event, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    using var scope = context.CreateScope();
    await _events.PublishAsync(new InvoiceRequested(@event.OrderId), scope.Context);
}
```

